  "$comment": "README system overview. A system map shows how the five systems connect (shared data, events, calls); below it each system is one lane (numbers match the UML sequence diagrams) across four zones: lane header, client UI (the Next.js app; the arrow names the Vercel API route it calls), the Google Cloud Run backend and third-party APIs. Lanes that depend on each other sit next to each other: `sharedStores` sit on the boundary of the lanes that share them, and `crossLinks` connect a lane's element to its neighbour's. `ref` cards stand for an element of a lane further away. Lane node and link y coordinates are relative to their lane, x is absolute; crossLinks give points as [x, laneId, y]. Every topology node is covered exactly once (`covers` / `resources`); every link maps to a topology edge, names its `edges`, or is marked `declarative` with the reason. Run `npm run docs:architecture` after editing.",
  "eyebrow": "YUQI.SITE · SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE",
  "title": "Portfolio Platform",
  "subtitle": "The map shows how the five systems connect; each lane below shows how one of them works, from the Next.js UI to Cloud Run and the data it owns.",
  "description": "System design overview of yuqi.site. A system map shows how Ask AI, content publishing, notifications, visitor analytics and the MCP tools connect, and the lanes below repeat those links in place: the pgvector RAG store sits on the boundary between Ask AI, which reads it, and publishing, which writes it; publishing writes the article and its outbox in one PostgreSQL transaction and an outbox relay hands content.notification.* to the notifications Kafka; analytics alerts call the notification service directly. Each lane shows its Next.js UI and the Vercel API route it calls, its Google Cloud Run services next to the PostgreSQL, Redis and OpenSearch stores they own, its Kafka topics and third-party APIs, followed by the Kubernetes exit-path decision record and platform concerns.",
